/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 2 Chronicles chapters 11-15
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("2chronicles", [
{n:11, name:"Rehoboam Fortifies Judah", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 11:16",verse:"And after them out of all the tribes of Israel such as set their hearts to seek the LORD God of Israel came to Jerusalem, to sacrifice unto the LORD God of their fathers."}, facts:["Did you know chapter 11 names fifteen cities Rehoboam fortified, from Bethlehem to Hebron, each stocked with shields, spears, food, oil, and wine?","Did you know Jeroboam ordained priests for the high places, and for the devils, and for the calves he had made, which is why the true priests and Levites fled south to Judah?","Did you know Rehoboam took eighteen wives and sixty concubines, and fathered twenty and eight sons and sixty daughters?"], questions:[
{q:"How many chosen men of war did Rehoboam assemble from Judah and Benjamin to fight Israel?", options:["180,000","400,000","800,000","120,000"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:1",verse:"And when Rehoboam was come to Jerusalem, he gathered of the house of Judah and Benjamin an hundred and fourscore thousand chosen men, which were warriors, to fight against Israel, that he might bring the kingdom again to Rehoboam.", insight:"Rehoboam reached for the sword before he reached for God. A big army felt like the answer, but heaven had already decided the matter."},
{q:"Which prophet brought God's word telling Rehoboam not to fight?", options:["Shemaiah the man of God","Ahijah the Shilonite","Iddo the seer","Oded the prophet"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:2",verse:"But the word of the LORD came to Shemaiah the man of God, saying,", insight:"God sent one man with one message at the moment war fever was highest. Hearing God's voice in time can save thousands of lives."},
{q:"What did the LORD say about the division of the kingdom?", options:["This thing is done of me","This thing will be undone in your lifetime","This thing came from your enemies alone","This thing must be avenged"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:4",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Ye shall not go up, nor fight against your brethren: return every man to his house: for this thing is done of me. And they obeyed the words of the LORD, and returned from going against Jeroboam.", insight:"God owned the painful split as His own doing. Sometimes the loss we want to fight is part of a plan we cannot yet see."},
{q:"How did Rehoboam and his people respond to Shemaiah's word?", options:["They obeyed the word of the LORD and returned every man to his house","They marched on Israel anyway","They imprisoned the prophet","They demanded a confirming sign"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:4",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Ye shall not go up, nor fight against your brethren: return every man to his house: for this thing is done of me. And they obeyed the words of the LORD, and returned from going against Jeroboam.", insight:"They did not just hear the word, they did it. Obedience turned a looming civil war into a quiet walk home."},
{q:"Where did Rehoboam dwell after turning back from war?", options:["In Jerusalem","In Shechem","In Tirzah","In Samaria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:5",verse:"And Rehoboam dwelt in Jerusalem, and built cities for defence in Judah.", insight:"He stayed in the city God had chosen. Holding your God given ground in obedience beats chasing a kingdom God has withheld."},
{q:"Which three cities head the list of those Rehoboam built for defense?", options:["Bethlehem, Etam, and Tekoa","Hebron, Zorah, and Aijalon","Lachish, Azekah, and Adoraim","Gath, Mareshah, and Ziph"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:6",verse:"He built even Bethlehem, and Etam, and Tekoa,", insight:"Bethlehem leads the list, a small town with a giant future. God often plants His biggest plans in the most ordinary places."},
{q:"What did Rehoboam put in every several city?", options:["Shields and spears","Bows and arrows","Chariots and horses","Battering rams"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:12",verse:"And in every several city he put shields and spears, and made them exceeding strong, having Judah and Benjamin on his side.", insight:"Practical preparation is not unbelief. Rehoboam armed his cities and, for a season, kept God's ways too."},
{q:"Besides captains, what did Rehoboam store in the strongholds?", options:["Store of victual, and of oil and wine","Gold and silver tribute","Incense for the temple","Scrolls of the law"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:11",verse:"And he fortified the strong holds, and put captains in them, and store of victual, and of oil and wine.", insight:"Wise leaders stock the storehouse before the siege, not during it. Foresight is stewardship in work clothes."},
{q:"Who resorted to Rehoboam out of all their coasts?", options:["The priests and the Levites","The mighty men of valor","The elders of the tribes","The king's counselors"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:13",verse:"And the priests and the Levites that were in all Israel resorted to him out of all their coasts.", insight:"Faithful servants follow God's order even when it costs them everything. The Levites left home to stay true."},
{q:"Why did the Levites leave their suburbs and their possession?", options:["Jeroboam and his sons had cast them off from the priest's office","Rehoboam promised them double portions","They feared the coming war","They wanted larger cities in Judah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:14",verse:"For the Levites left their suburbs and their possession, and came to Judah and Jerusalem: for Jeroboam and his sons had cast them off from executing the priest’s office unto the LORD:", insight:"When Jeroboam corrupted worship, the Levites walked away rather than serve a lie. Integrity sometimes means leaving a good post."},
{q:"For what did Jeroboam ordain his own priests?", options:["The high places, the devils, and the calves","The temple and the ark","The feasts and the sabbaths","The tithes and offerings"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:15",verse:"And he ordained him priests for the high places, and for the devils, and for the calves which he had made.", insight:"False worship always hires its own clergy. Jeroboam built a religion that served his politics, not God's truth."},
{q:"Who came to Jerusalem after the priests and Levites?", options:["Those from every tribe who set their hearts to seek the LORD","Deserters from Jeroboam's army","Traders from Egypt","Refugees fleeing famine"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:16",verse:"And after them out of all the tribes of Israel such as set their hearts to seek the LORD God of Israel came to Jerusalem, to sacrifice unto the LORD God of their fathers.", insight:"Revival follows the faithful. When the Levites moved south, ordinary seekers from every tribe moved with them."},
{q:"For how many years did the faithful strengthen Rehoboam's kingdom?", options:["Three years","Seven years","Ten years","Twelve years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:17",verse:"So they strengthened the kingdom of Judah, and made Rehoboam the son of Solomon strong, three years: for three years they walked in the way of David and Solomon.", insight:"Three years of walking in David's way made Judah strong. Faithfulness compounds, even in short seasons."},
{q:"In whose way did Judah walk during those three years?", options:["The way of David and Solomon","The way of Jeroboam","The way of the Philistines","The way of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:17",verse:"So they strengthened the kingdom of Judah, and made Rehoboam the son of Solomon strong, three years: for three years they walked in the way of David and Solomon.", insight:"The measuring line was David's devotion. Every generation must choose whose footsteps it will follow."},
{q:"Which wife did Rehoboam love above all his wives and concubines?", options:["Maachah the daughter of Absalom","Mahalath the daughter of Jerimoth","Abihail the daughter of Eliab","Naamah the Ammonitess"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:21",verse:"And Rehoboam loved Maachah the daughter of Absalom above all his wives and his concubines: (for he took eighteen wives, and threescore concubines; and begat twenty and eight sons, and threescore daughters.)", insight:"A king's private loves shape a kingdom's public future. Rehoboam's crowded household was already sowing tomorrow's trouble."},
{q:"How many sons and daughters did Rehoboam beget?", options:["Twenty and eight sons and sixty daughters","Twenty and two sons and sixteen daughters","Twelve sons and forty daughters","Seventy sons and thirty daughters"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:21",verse:"And Rehoboam loved Maachah the daughter of Absalom above all his wives and his concubines: (for he took eighteen wives, and threescore concubines; and begat twenty and eight sons, and threescore daughters.)", insight:"A huge family looked like royal success, yet numbers never made Rehoboam wise. Blessing without obedience still drifts."},
{q:"Whom did Rehoboam make chief, to be ruler among his brethren?", options:["Abijah the son of Maachah","Jeush the son of Mahalath","Zaham the son of Mahalath","Attai the son of Maachah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:22",verse:"And Rehoboam made Abijah the son of Maachah the chief, to be ruler among his brethren: for he thought to make him king.", insight:"Rehoboam named his successor openly and early. A clear plan for what comes next is part of leading well."},
{q:"How did Rehoboam deal wisely with his many sons?", options:["He dispersed them through the fenced cities with victual in abundance","He sent them all to Egypt for training","He divided the kingdom among them","He made them all priests"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:23",verse:"And he dealt wisely, and dispersed of all his children throughout all the countries of Judah and Benjamin, unto every fenced city: and he gave them victual in abundance. And he desired many wives.", insight:"Spreading his sons across strong cities kept peace in the family and strength in the kingdom. Wise placement prevents rivalry."},
{q:"What did Rehoboam give his dispersed sons in abundance?", options:["Victual","Gold","Horses","Chariots"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:23",verse:"And he dealt wisely, and dispersed of all his children throughout all the countries of Judah and Benjamin, unto every fenced city: and he gave them victual in abundance. And he desired many wives.", insight:"He provided for them generously. Provision is one quiet way a father keeps his house united."},
{q:"Which two tribes remained on Rehoboam's part?", options:["Judah and Benjamin","Judah and Ephraim","Judah and Manasseh","Judah and Simeon"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 11:12",verse:"And in every several city he put shields and spears, and made them exceeding strong, having Judah and Benjamin on his side.", insight:"God preserved two tribes for David's line. Even in division, He kept His covenant promise alive."}
]},
{n:12, name:"Shishak Invades", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 12:7",verse:"And when the LORD saw that they humbled themselves, the word of the LORD came to Shemaiah, saying, They have humbled themselves; therefore I will not destroy them, but I will grant them some deliverance; and my wrath shall not be poured out upon Jerusalem by the hand of Shishak."}, facts:["Did you know Shishak's invasion is confirmed outside the Bible? Pharaoh Shoshenq I carved his victory, listing captured towns of Judah, on the walls of the Karnak temple in Egypt.","Did you know Shishak carried off the shields of gold Solomon had made, and Rehoboam replaced them with bronze? The guard bore them whenever the king entered the temple.","Did you know Rehoboam was forty and one when he began to reign, ruled seventeen years in Jerusalem, and his mother's name was Naamah the Ammonitess?"], questions:[
{q:"When did Rehoboam forsake the law of the LORD?", options:["When he had established the kingdom and strengthened himself","In the first year of his reign","Right after Solomon's death","During the war with Jeroboam"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:1",verse:"And it came to pass, when Rehoboam had established the kingdom, and had strengthened himself, he forsook the law of the LORD, and all Israel with him.", insight:"Strength became his snare. Success often tempts us to forget the God who gave it."},
{q:"In which year of Rehoboam did Shishak king of Egypt come against Jerusalem?", options:["The fifth year","The third year","The seventh year","The tenth year"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:2",verse:"And it came to pass, that in the fifth year of king Rehoboam Shishak king of Egypt came up against Jerusalem, because they had transgressed against the LORD,", insight:"Judgment followed apostasy within a few short years. God's patience is real, but it is not endless."},
{q:"How many chariots did Shishak bring?", options:["Twelve hundred","Six hundred","Three hundred","Ten thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:3",verse:"With twelve hundred chariots, and threescore thousand horsemen: and the people were without number that came with him out of Egypt; the Lubim, the Sukkiims, and the Ethiopians.", insight:"Egypt's might looked overwhelming on paper, yet God needed no army to humble Judah. He simply used one."},
{q:"Which three peoples came with Shishak out of Egypt?", options:["The Lubims, the Sukkiims, and the Ethiopians","The Philistines, the Moabites, and the Ammonites","The Syrians, the Hittites, and the Amorites","The Midianites, the Amalekites, and the Edomites"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:3",verse:"With twelve hundred chariots, and threescore thousand horsemen: and the people were without number that came with him out of Egypt; the Lubim, the Sukkiims, and the Ethiopians.", insight:"Egypt emptied its allies onto little Judah. When we forsake God, our enemies sense the opening."},
{q:"What did Shishak take before he came to Jerusalem?", options:["The fenced cities which pertained to Judah","Only the temple vessels","The city of Hebron","The flocks of Benjamin"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:4",verse:"And he took the fenced cities which pertained to Judah, and came to Jerusalem.", insight:"The very strongholds Rehoboam built could not save a backslidden kingdom. Walls without God are just scenery."},
{q:"Which prophet came to Rehoboam and the princes gathered at Jerusalem because of Shishak?", options:["Shemaiah","Iddo","Ahijah","Oded"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:5",verse:"Then came Shemaiah the prophet to Rehoboam, and to the princes of Judah, that were gathered together to Jerusalem because of Shishak, and said unto them, Thus saith the LORD, Ye have forsaken me, and therefore have I also left you in the hand of Shishak.", insight:"The same prophet who once stopped a civil war now announced judgment. God's messengers speak in every season, welcome or not."},
{q:"What did Shemaiah say the LORD had done because they forsook Him?", options:["Left them in the hand of Shishak","Sent fire from heaven","Shut the temple doors","Given them to Jeroboam"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:5",verse:"Then came Shemaiah the prophet to Rehoboam, and to the princes of Judah, that were gathered together to Jerusalem because of Shishak, and said unto them, Thus saith the LORD, Ye have forsaken me, and therefore have I also left you in the hand of Shishak.", insight:"The punishment fit the sin. They left God, so God left them to the mercy of their enemy."},
{q:"How did the princes and the king answer Shemaiah?", options:["They humbled themselves and said, The LORD is righteous","They fled to the fenced cities","They offered Shishak tribute","They mustered the army"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:6",verse:"Whereupon the princes of Israel and the king humbled themselves; and they said, The LORD is righteous.", insight:"Confession came before deliverance. Agreeing that God is right is the first step back."},
{q:"What did the LORD grant Judah when they humbled themselves?", options:["Some deliverance","Total victory over Egypt","The return of every captive","A brand new covenant"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:7",verse:"And when the LORD saw that they humbled themselves, the word of the LORD came to Shemaiah, saying, They have humbled themselves; therefore I will not destroy them, but I will grant them some deliverance; and my wrath shall not be poured out upon Jerusalem by the hand of Shishak.", insight:"God did not erase every consequence, but He showed mercy. Some deliverance from God is better than none."},
{q:"Why did God still let Judah serve Shishak for a time?", options:["That they might know God's service and the service of foreign kingdoms","Because Shishak was stronger than God","To punish the Levites","To fulfill Jeroboam's prophecy"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:8",verse:"Nevertheless they shall be his servants; that they may know my service, and the service of the kingdoms of the countries.", insight:"God let them taste the difference between serving Him and serving a tyrant. Contrast is a powerful teacher."},
{q:"What did Shishak take from the house of the LORD and the king's house?", options:["The treasures, including the shields of gold Solomon had made","The ark of the covenant","The brazen altar","The temple scrolls"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:9",verse:"So Shishak king of Egypt came up against Jerusalem, and took away the treasures of the house of the LORD, and the treasures of the king’s house; he took all: he carried away also the shields of gold which Solomon had made.", insight:"Solomon's glory became Egypt's plunder in a single generation. Wealth built without lasting obedience cannot be kept."},
{q:"What did Rehoboam make to replace the shields of gold?", options:["Shields of brass","Shields of silver","Shields of wood","Shields of iron"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:10",verse:"Instead of which king Rehoboam made shields of brass, and committed them to the hands of the chief of the guard, that kept the entrance of the king’s house.", insight:"Brass replaced gold, a picture of diminished glory. Sin always trades down what grace had built up."},
{q:"To whom did Rehoboam commit the brass shields?", options:["The chief of the guard that kept the entrance of the king's house","The Levites in the temple","The captains of the fenced cities","The priests of the high places"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:10",verse:"Instead of which king Rehoboam made shields of brass, and committed them to the hands of the chief of the guard, that kept the entrance of the king’s house.", insight:"Even the replacements were guarded with ceremony. We often polish substitutes instead of repenting and recovering the real thing."},
{q:"When did the guard fetch the shields?", options:["When the king entered into the house of the LORD","At every new moon feast","When Shishak threatened again","At Abijah's coronation"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:11",verse:"And when the king entered into the house of the LORD, the guard came and fetched them, and brought them again into the guard chamber.", insight:"Rehoboam kept up temple appearances with borrowed glory. Ritual without repentance is theater."},
{q:"What happened when Rehoboam humbled himself?", options:["The wrath of the LORD turned from him, and Judah was not destroyed altogether","Shishak returned all the treasure","Jeroboam made peace with Judah","The golden shields were recovered"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:12",verse:"And when he humbled himself, the wrath of the LORD turned from him that he would not destroy him altogether: and also in Judah things went well.", insight:"Humility turned away total destruction. God answers a humbled heart faster than we expect."},
{q:"How old was Rehoboam when he began to reign, and how long did he reign?", options:["Forty and one years old, seventeen years","Thirty years old, forty years","Twenty and five years old, twenty and two years","Sixteen years old, fifty and two years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:13",verse:"So king Rehoboam strengthened himself in Jerusalem, and reigned: for Rehoboam was one and forty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city which the LORD had chosen out of all the tribes of Israel, to put his name there. And his mother’s name was Naamah an Ammonitess.", insight:"Seventeen years gave him time to finish well, yet he did not. Length of reign matters less than direction of heart."},
{q:"What was the name of Rehoboam's mother?", options:["Naamah the Ammonitess","Maachah the daughter of Absalom","Michaiah the daughter of Uriel","Abihail the daughter of Eliab"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:13",verse:"So king Rehoboam strengthened himself in Jerusalem, and reigned: for Rehoboam was one and forty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city which the LORD had chosen out of all the tribes of Israel, to put his name there. And his mother’s name was Naamah an Ammonitess.", insight:"Scripture names her twice, a quiet reminder that a king's home shapes a king's heart."},
{q:"Why did Rehoboam do evil?", options:["Because he prepared not his heart to seek the LORD","Because Jeroboam forced him","Because the prophets failed him","Because of the famine"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:14",verse:"And he did evil, because he prepared not his heart to seek the LORD.", insight:"The problem was an unprepared heart, not bad circumstances. Seeking God is a deliberate choice, not an accident."},
{q:"In whose records were the acts of Rehoboam, first and last, written?", options:["The book of Shemaiah the prophet, and of Iddo the seer","The books of Nathan and Gad","The chronicles of Ahijah and Jehu","The book of Samuel"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:15",verse:"Now the acts of Rehoboam, first and last, are they not written in the book of Shemaiah the prophet, and of Iddo the seer concerning genealogies? And there were wars between Rehoboam and Jeroboam continually.", insight:"Every reign is recorded somewhere. God keeps the books even when men forget."},
{q:"Who reigned in Rehoboam's stead?", options:["Abijah his son","Asa his son","Jeroboam","Maachah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 12:16",verse:"And Rehoboam slept with his fathers, and was buried in the city of David: and Abijah his son reigned in his stead.", insight:"The kingdom passed to his son as God had promised David. God's covenant outlasts any one king's failures."}
]},
{n:13, name:"Abijah's Stand", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 13:18",verse:"Thus the children of Israel were brought under at that time, and the children of Judah prevailed, because they relied upon the LORD God of their fathers."}, facts:["Did you know Abijah preached a full sermon to Jeroboam's army from Mount Zemaraim before a sword was drawn, one of the longest battlefield addresses in Scripture?","Did you know Judah's priests burned sacrifices every morning and evening, set the showbread in order, and lit the golden lamps nightly, exactly as the law commanded?","Did you know 500,000 of Israel's chosen men fell in a single day, making this one of the deadliest battles recorded in the Bible?"], questions:[
{q:"In which year of king Jeroboam did Abijah begin to reign over Judah?", options:["The eighteenth year","The fifteenth year","The twentieth year","The twelfth year"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:1",verse:"Now in the eighteenth year of king Jeroboam began Abijah to reign over Judah.", insight:"Abijah's reign began deep into Jeroboam's rule. God raises His servants in whatever season He pleases."},
{q:"How long did Abijah reign in Jerusalem?", options:["Three years","Seventeen years","Forty and one years","Twenty and five years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:2",verse:"He reigned three years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name also was Michaiah the daughter of Uriel of Gibeah. And there was war between Abijah and Jeroboam.", insight:"Three short years, yet one of them held Judah's greatest battlefield testimony. Impact is not measured in years."},
{q:"How many chosen men did Abijah set in battle array?", options:["Four hundred thousand","Eight hundred thousand","One hundred and eighty thousand","Three hundred thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:3",verse:"And Abijah set the battle in array with an army of valiant men of war, even four hundred thousand chosen men: Jeroboam also set the battle in array against him with eight hundred thousand chosen men, being mighty men of valour.", insight:"Abijah was outnumbered two to one and still stood his ground. Courage counts the cause, not the crowd."},
{q:"How many chosen men did Jeroboam array against him?", options:["Eight hundred thousand","Four hundred thousand","A thousand thousand","Sixty thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:3",verse:"And Abijah set the battle in array with an army of valiant men of war, even four hundred thousand chosen men: Jeroboam also set the battle in array against him with eight hundred thousand chosen men, being mighty men of valour.", insight:"Jeroboam trusted in double the swords. Numbers impress men, but they do not impress God."},
{q:"From which mount did Abijah address Jeroboam and all Israel?", options:["Mount Zemaraim in Mount Ephraim","Mount Gerizim","Mount Moriah","Mount Carmel"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:4",verse:"And Abijah stood up upon mount Zemaraim, which is in mount Ephraim, and said, Hear me, thou Jeroboam, and all Israel;", insight:"Abijah preached from the edge of enemy territory, bold as a lion. Truth does not need home ground to stand tall."},
{q:"By what kind of covenant did Abijah say God gave the kingdom to David forever?", options:["A covenant of salt","A covenant of blood","A covenant of iron","A covenant of oil"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:5",verse:"Ought ye not to know that the LORD God of Israel gave the kingdom over Israel to David for ever, even to him and to his sons by a covenant of salt?", insight:"Salt spoke of permanence and preservation. God's promise to David would not spoil or fade."},
{q:"How did Abijah describe the men gathered to Jeroboam?", options:["Vain men, the children of Belial","Mighty men of valor","Faithful servants of God","Priests and Levites"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:7",verse:"And there are gathered unto him vain men, the children of Belial, and have strengthened themselves against Rehoboam the son of Solomon, when Rehoboam was young and tenderhearted, and could not withstand them.", insight:"Abijah named rebellion's recruits plainly. Flattering evil never weakens it."},
{q:"What did Abijah say stood with Jeroboam's great multitude?", options:["Golden calves which Jeroboam made for gods","Baal and Ashtaroth","The host of heaven","The idols of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:8",verse:"And now ye think to withstand the kingdom of the LORD in the hand of the sons of David; and ye be a great multitude, and there are with you golden calves, which Jeroboam made you for gods.", insight:"Jeroboam's calves were politics dressed as religion. Abijah stripped the golden paint off for all to see."},
{q:"What price did Jeroboam set for becoming one of his priests?", options:["A young bullock and seven rams","Ten shekels of silver","A lamb and a dove","Thirty pieces of silver"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:9",verse:"Have ye not cast out the priests of the LORD, the sons of Aaron, and the Levites, and have made you priests after the manner of the nations of other lands? so that whosoever cometh to consecrate himself with a young bullock and seven rams, the same may be a priest of them that are no gods.", insight:"Jeroboam sold the priesthood to anyone with livestock. When ministry is for sale, worship is already lost."},
{q:"Who did Abijah say ministered to the LORD in Judah?", options:["The sons of Aaron, with the Levites waiting on their business","Whoever brought a bullock and seven rams","The elders of the tribes","The captains of the army"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:10",verse:"But as for us, the LORD is our God, and we have not forsaken him; and the priests, which minister unto the LORD, are the sons of Aaron, and the Levites wait upon their business:", insight:"Judah kept God's appointed order. True worship follows God's pattern, not man's convenience."},
{q:"What did Judah's priests burn to the LORD every morning and every evening?", options:["Burnt sacrifices and sweet incense","Peace offerings and drink offerings","Firstfruits and tithes","Sin offerings alone"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:11",verse:"And they burn unto the LORD every morning and every evening burnt sacrifices and sweet incense: the shewbread also set they in order upon the pure table; and the candlestick of gold with the lamps thereof, to burn every evening: for we keep the charge of the LORD our God; but ye have forsaken him.", insight:"Steady daily worship anchored Judah's courage. The battle was won at the altar before it was won on the field."},
{q:"Whom did Abijah call Judah's captain?", options:["God himself","Abijah","The high priest","The eldest captain"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:12",verse:"And, behold, God himself is with us for our captain, and his priests with sounding trumpets to cry alarm against you. O children of Israel, fight ye not against the LORD God of your fathers; for ye shall not prosper.", insight:"With God as captain, Judah's army had heaven's general. No earthly commander outranks Him."},
{q:"What warning did Abijah give the children of Israel?", options:["Fight not against the LORD God of your fathers, for ye shall not prosper","Surrender your cities and live","Pay yearly tribute to Jerusalem","Return to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:12",verse:"And, behold, God himself is with us for our captain, and his priests with sounding trumpets to cry alarm against you. O children of Israel, fight ye not against the LORD God of your fathers; for ye shall not prosper.", insight:"Abijah warned before he fought. Even in war, God's people plead with their enemies to turn."},
{q:"What did Jeroboam do while Abijah was speaking?", options:["He set an ambush behind Judah","He withdrew his army","He sent for reinforcements","He offered a truce"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:13",verse:"But Jeroboam caused an ambushment to come about behind them: so they were before Judah, and the ambushment was behind them.", insight:"Jeroboam answered a sermon with treachery. When truth corners a man, he either repents or schemes."},
{q:"What did Judah do when they saw the battle was before and behind?", options:["They cried unto the LORD, and the priests sounded the trumpets","They broke ranks and fled","They surrendered to Jeroboam","They cast lots for direction"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:14",verse:"And when Judah looked back, behold, the battle was before and behind: and they cried unto the LORD, and the priests sounded with the trumpets.", insight:"Trapped between two armies, they cried upward instead of giving up. Prayer is the weapon that works when you are surrounded."},
{q:"What happened as the men of Judah shouted?", options:["God smote Jeroboam and all Israel before Abijah and Judah","The ambush turned back","Jeroboam surrendered","A storm scattered both armies"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:15",verse:"Then the men of Judah gave a shout: and as the men of Judah shouted, it came to pass, that God smote Jeroboam and all Israel before Abijah and Judah.", insight:"The shout was faith made audible. God fought the battle Judah could never have won alone."},
{q:"How many of Israel fell down slain that day?", options:["Five hundred thousand chosen men","Four hundred thousand","Eighty thousand","A thousand thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:17",verse:"And Abijah and his people slew them with a great slaughter: so there fell down slain of Israel five hundred thousand chosen men.", insight:"The slaughter was staggering, a sober reminder that fighting against God is the costliest war of all."},
{q:"Why did the children of Judah prevail?", options:["Because they relied upon the LORD God of their fathers","Because they had better weapons","Because of their greater numbers","Because of Jeroboam's mistakes"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:18",verse:"Thus the children of Israel were brought under at that time, and the children of Judah prevailed, because they relied upon the LORD God of their fathers.", insight:"The whole victory is summed up in one word, reliance. Trust in God was Judah's true weapon."},
{q:"Which cities did Abijah take from Jeroboam?", options:["Bethel, Jeshanah, and Ephrain, with the towns thereof","Shechem, Tirzah, and Samaria","Dan, Bethel, and Gilgal","Hebron, Debir, and Lachish"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:19",verse:"And Abijah pursued after Jeroboam, and took cities from him, Bethel with the towns thereof, and Jeshanah with the towns thereof, and Ephrain with the towns thereof.", insight:"Victory won back lost ground, including Bethel where a calf once stood. God restores what rebellion stole."},
{q:"How many wives, sons, and daughters did Abijah have?", options:["Fourteen wives, twenty and two sons, and sixteen daughters","Eighteen wives, twenty and eight sons, and sixty daughters","Seven wives, twelve sons, and ten daughters","Ten wives, thirty sons, and twenty daughters"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 13:21",verse:"But Abijah waxed mighty, and married fourteen wives, and begat twenty and two sons, and sixteen daughters.", insight:"Like his father, Abijah multiplied wives, the old royal habit. Even victorious kings carried the family's old weakness."}
]},
{n:14, name:"Asa's Victory", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 14:11",verse:"And Asa cried unto the LORD his God, and said, LORD, it is nothing with thee to help, whether with many, or with them that have no power: help us, O LORD our God; for we rest on thee, and in thy name we go against this multitude. O LORD, thou art our God; let not man prevail against thee."}, facts:["Did you know Asa used ten peaceful years to build walled cities with towers, gates, and bars, preparing in peacetime for the wars to come?","Did you know Asa's prayer before facing a million Ethiopians is one of the boldest short prayers in the Bible: it is nothing with thee to help, whether with many, or with them that have no power?","Did you know Judah smote the cities around Gerar because the fear of the LORD came upon them, and took sheep and camels in abundance from the tents of cattle?"], questions:[
{q:"How long was the land quiet at the beginning of Asa's reign?", options:["Ten years","Three years","Seven years","Twenty years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:1",verse:"So Abijah slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of David: and Asa his son reigned in his stead. In his days the land was quiet ten years.", insight:"God gave Asa a decade of peace. Quiet seasons are God's gift for building, not for coasting."},
{q:"What kind of king was Asa in the eyes of the LORD?", options:["He did that which was good and right","He did evil continually","He was lukewarm","He followed Jeroboam's way"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:2",verse:"And Asa did that which was good and right in the eyes of the LORD his God:", insight:"Scripture's verdict on Asa is warm and plain. A good heart toward God is the highest praise a king can earn."},
{q:"What did Asa take away first in his reforms?", options:["The altars of the strange gods, the high places, the images, and the groves","The golden calves of Dan and Bethel","The temple vessels","The city walls"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:3",verse:"For he took away the altars of the strange gods, and the high places, and brake down the images, and cut down the groves:", insight:"Asa started with the idols. You cannot seek God fully while keeping rivals for your heart."},
{q:"What did Asa command Judah to do?", options:["Seek the LORD and do the law and the commandment","Build more fenced cities","Pay tribute to Egypt","Number the fighting men"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:4",verse:"And commanded Judah to seek the LORD God of their fathers, and to do the law and the commandment.", insight:"Reform was not only tearing down but building up. Asa replaced idolatry with obedience, not with emptiness."},
{q:"Why was Asa able to build fenced cities in peace?", options:["The LORD had given him rest, and he had no war in those years","Egypt protected Judah","Jeroboam was dead","The Philistines paid tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:6",verse:"And he built fenced cities in Judah: for the land had rest, and he had no war in those years; because the LORD had given him rest.", insight:"Rest is God's doing. Asa used his peaceful years to strengthen the kingdom instead of wasting them."},
{q:"What reason did Asa give for building while the land was yet before them?", options:["They had sought the LORD, who had given them rest on every side","Egypt was weak","The treasury was full","The army was large"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:7",verse:"Therefore he said unto Judah, Let us build these cities, and make about them walls, and towers, gates, and bars, while the land is yet before us; because we have sought the LORD our God, we have sought him, and he hath given us rest on every side. So they built and prospered.", insight:"Asa traced his peace to its source. Gratitude that names God keeps prosperity from breeding pride."},
{q:"How large was Asa's army from Judah and Benjamin?", options:["Three hundred thousand from Judah, and two hundred and eighty thousand from Benjamin","Four hundred thousand and eight hundred thousand","One hundred and eighty thousand","A thousand thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:8",verse:"And Asa had an army of men that bare targets and spears, out of Judah three hundred thousand; and out of Benjamin, that bare shields and drew bows, two hundred and fourscore thousand: all these were mighty men of valour.", insight:"Nearly six hundred thousand mighty men stood ready. God had blessed Judah's faithfulness with real strength."},
{q:"Who came out against Asa with a host of a thousand thousand?", options:["Zerah the Ethiopian","Shishak king of Egypt","Jeroboam","Baasha king of Israel"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:9",verse:"And there came out against them Zerah the Ethiopian with an host of a thousand thousand, and three hundred chariots; and came unto Mareshah.", insight:"A million man army marched on Judah. The test of Asa's reforms came in the form of overwhelming odds."},
{q:"How many chariots did Zerah bring?", options:["Three hundred","Twelve hundred","Six hundred","One thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:9",verse:"And there came out against them Zerah the Ethiopian with an host of a thousand thousand, and three hundred chariots; and came unto Mareshah.", insight:"Chariots were the tanks of the ancient world. Judah faced the finest military technology of its day."},
{q:"Where did Asa set the battle in array against Zerah?", options:["The valley of Zephathah at Mareshah","Mount Zemaraim","The valley of Elah","Before the gates of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:10",verse:"Then Asa went out against him, and they set the battle in array in the valley of Zephathah at Mareshah.", insight:"Asa met the giant army in the open valley. Faith does not always wait behind walls."},
{q:"How did Asa's prayer describe God's power to help?", options:["It is nothing with thee to help, whether with many or with them that have no power","God only helps the strong","God helps those who never pray","Victory belongs to the largest army"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:11",verse:"And Asa cried unto the LORD his God, and said, LORD, it is nothing with thee to help, whether with many, or with them that have no power: help us, O LORD our God; for we rest on thee, and in thy name we go against this multitude. O LORD, thou art our God; let not man prevail against thee.", insight:"Asa's confidence rested in God's ease, not Judah's numbers. To the Almighty, a million men are no harder than ten."},
{q:"What did Asa take away out of all the cities of Judah?", options:["The high places and the images","The walls and the gates","The markets and the wells","The palaces and the towers"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:5",verse:"Also he took away out of all the cities of Judah the high places and the images: and the kingdom was quiet before him.", insight:"Asa's cleanup reached every city, not just Jerusalem. Reform that stays in the capital never changes the country."},
{q:"Before whom did the LORD smite the Ethiopians?", options:["Before Asa, and before Judah","Before Zerah's chariots","Before the men of Gerar","Before the Egyptians"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:12",verse:"So the LORD smote the Ethiopians before Asa and before Judah; and the Ethiopians fled.", insight:"Scripture credits the victory to God's presence with His people. When God goes before us, enemies fall before us."},
{q:"Unto what place did Asa and his people pursue the fleeing Ethiopians?", options:["Gerar","Mareshah","Jerusalem","Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:13",verse:"And Asa and the people that were with him pursued them unto Gerar: and the Ethiopians were overthrown, that they could not recover themselves; for they were destroyed before the LORD, and before his host; and they carried away very much spoil.", insight:"They chased the enemy all the way to Philistine country. God gave not mere survival but complete pursuit."},
{q:"What did Judah carry away from the defeated Ethiopians?", options:["Very much spoil","Only their three hundred chariots","Their idols of gold","Nothing; they left it all"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:13",verse:"And Asa and the people that were with him pursued them unto Gerar: and the Ethiopians were overthrown, that they could not recover themselves; for they were destroyed before the LORD, and before his host; and they carried away very much spoil.", insight:"God turned the invader's wealth into Judah's provision. The enemy's plan became Judah's supply."},
{q:"Why could Judah spoil the cities round about Gerar?", options:["The fear of the LORD came upon them","The cities opened their gates in friendship","Asa paid their governors","The Ethiopians defended them"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:14",verse:"And they smote all the cities round about Gerar; for the fear of the LORD came upon them: and they spoiled all the cities; for there was exceeding much spoil in them.", insight:"God fought ahead of His people with holy fear. Sometimes the victory is won before we arrive."},
{q:"What did Judah take from the tents of cattle?", options:["Sheep and camels in abundance","Horses and mules","Oxen and asses","Silver and gold only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:15",verse:"They smote also the tents of cattle, and carried away sheep and camels in abundance, and returned to Jerusalem.", insight:"The spoil was practical wealth for a farming people. God's blessings fit the hands that receive them."},
{q:"Where did Asa and the people return after the victory?", options:["To Jerusalem","To Mareshah","To Hebron","To Gerar"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:15",verse:"They smote also the tents of cattle, and carried away sheep and camels in abundance, and returned to Jerusalem.", insight:"They came home to God's city with full hands. Every victory should end in worship at the right place."},
{q:"Who was Asa's father?", options:["Abijah","Rehoboam","Solomon","Jeroboam"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:1",verse:"So Abijah slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of David: and Asa his son reigned in his stead. In his days the land was quiet ten years.", insight:"Asa followed a father known for one great victory and many wives. Asa kept the victory and left the weakness behind."},
{q:"How did the men of Benjamin serve in Asa's army?", options:["They bare shields and drew bows","They bare targets and spears","They drove the chariots","They sounded the trumpets"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 14:8",verse:"And Asa had an army of men that bare targets and spears, out of Judah three hundred thousand; and out of Benjamin, that bare shields and drew bows, two hundred and fourscore thousand: all these were mighty men of valour.", insight:"Judah and Benjamin each served in their own strength. God uses different gifts side by side in one army."}
]},
{n:15, name:"Covenant Renewal", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 15:12",verse:"And they entered into a covenant to seek the LORD God of their fathers with all their heart and with all their soul;"}, facts:["Did you know Azariah described faithless times when nation was destroyed of nation, and city of city, because God vexed the land with all adversity?","Did you know men from Ephraim, Manasseh, and Simeon fell to Asa in abundance when they saw that the LORD his God was with him?","Did you know Asa removed his own mother Maachah from being queen because she made an idol in a grove? He cut it down, stamped it, and burned it at the brook Kidron."], questions:[
{q:"Upon whom did the Spirit of God come at the start of the chapter?", options:["Azariah the son of Oded","Shemaiah the prophet","Iddo the seer","Hanani the seer"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:1",verse:"And the Spirit of God came upon Azariah the son of Oded:", insight:"God raised a prophet for the moment of victory. Triumph is when we most need a word from God."},
{q:"What promise did Azariah bring to Asa?", options:["The LORD is with you, while ye be with him","The LORD will never test you again","The LORD has forgotten your sins","The LORD will give you Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:2",verse:"And he went out to meet Asa, and said unto him, Hear ye me, Asa, and all Judah and Benjamin; The LORD is with you, while ye be with him; and if ye seek him, he will be found of you; but if ye forsake him, he will forsake you.", insight:"God's presence is promised, not presumed. The condition is ours to keep; the presence is His to give."},
{q:"What warning came with Azariah's promise?", options:["If ye forsake him, he will forsake you","If ye sin once, he will destroy you","If ye forget, he will forget David","If ye fear, he will flee"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:2",verse:"And he went out to meet Asa, and said unto him, Hear ye me, Asa, and all Judah and Benjamin; The LORD is with you, while ye be with him; and if ye seek him, he will be found of you; but if ye forsake him, he will forsake you.", insight:"The warning is the fence around the promise. God's faithfulness does not cancel our responsibility."},
{q:"What three things had Israel long been without?", options:["The true God, a teaching priest, and the law","A king, an army, and walls","Gold, silver, and vessels","Prophets, singers, and porters"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:3",verse:"Now for a long season Israel hath been without the true God, and without a teaching priest, and without law.", insight:"A nation can keep religion's shell and miss its heart. Without truth, teaching, and law, Israel drifted."},
{q:"What did Azariah say Israel did when trouble came?", options:["They turned unto the LORD and sought him, and he was found of them","They fled to Egypt","They made new idols","They hired foreign armies"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:4",verse:"But when they in their trouble did turn unto the LORD God of Israel, and sought him, he was found of them.", insight:"Trouble drove them back to God, and He received them. No one is too far gone to be found."},
{q:"What did Azariah say about peace in those troubled times?", options:["There was no peace to him that went out, nor to him that came in","Every city enjoyed perfect peace","Only Jerusalem knew trouble","The roads were safe for travelers"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:5",verse:"And in those times there was no peace to him that went out, nor to him that came in, but great vexations were upon all the inhabitants of the countries.", insight:"When a land forgets God, even daily coming and going turns fearful. Peace is a fruit of faithfulness."},
{q:"How did Azariah describe the turmoil among the nations?", options:["Nation was destroyed of nation, and city of city","Kingdom helped kingdom in peace","City traded with city in plenty","Nation taught nation the law"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:6",verse:"And nation was destroyed of nation, and city of city: for God did vex them with all adversity.", insight:"God let the nations vex one another as judgment. A world without God soon turns on itself."},
{q:"What charge did Azariah give Asa and the people?", options:["Be ye strong, and let not your hands be weak","Be ye silent, and wait","Be ye afraid, and hide","Be ye rich, and build"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:7",verse:"Be ye strong therefore, and let not your hands be weak: for your work shall be rewarded.", insight:"Strength here means steady hands for God's work. Discouragement is often the enemy's first weapon."},
{q:"What reason did Azariah give for being strong?", options:["Your work shall be rewarded","Your enemies shall flee","Your name shall be great","Your sons shall reign"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:7",verse:"Be ye strong therefore, and let not your hands be weak: for your work shall be rewarded.", insight:"God notices faithful work. The promise of reward turns duty into hope."},
{q:"What did Asa do when he heard the prophecy?", options:["He took courage, put away the abominable idols, and renewed the altar of the LORD","He built a new palace","He numbered the people","He sent gifts to the prophet"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:8",verse:"And when Asa heard these words, and the prophecy of Oded the prophet, he took courage, and put away the abominable idols out of all the land of Judah and Benjamin, and out of the cities which he had taken from mount Ephraim, and renewed the altar of the LORD, that was before the porch of the LORD.", insight:"Courage came first, then cleanup. Hearing God's word should move our hands, not just our hearts."},
{q:"From which tribes did strangers fall to Asa in abundance?", options:["Ephraim, Manasseh, and Simeon","Dan, Naphtali, and Asher","Gad, Reuben, and Zebulun","Issachar, Judah, and Benjamin"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:9",verse:"And he gathered all Judah and Benjamin, and the strangers with them out of Ephraim and Manasseh, and out of Simeon: for they fell to him out of Israel in abundance, when they saw that the LORD his God was with him.", insight:"Godliness attracts. When people saw God with Asa, they crossed borders to join him."},
{q:"When and where did the people gather for the covenant?", options:["At Jerusalem, in the third month of Asa's fifteenth year","At Hebron, in the seventh month of his tenth year","At Shechem, in the first month of his twentieth year","At Bethel, in the ninth month of his fifth year"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:10",verse:"So they gathered themselves together at Jerusalem in the third month, in the fifteenth year of the reign of Asa.", insight:"They set a time and a place for commitment. Covenant with God deserves a deliberate appointment."},
{q:"What did they offer to the LORD from the spoil?", options:["Seven hundred oxen and seven thousand sheep","Seven oxen and seventy sheep","A thousand bullocks and ten thousand lambs","Gold and silver vessels"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:11",verse:"And they offered unto the LORD the same time, of the spoil which they had brought, seven hundred oxen and seven thousand sheep.", insight:"They gave back to God from what God had given them. Gratitude turns spoil into sacrifice."},
{q:"What covenant did they enter into?", options:["To seek the LORD God of their fathers with all their heart and with all their soul","To never go to war again","To rebuild the temple walls","To pay yearly tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:12",verse:"And they entered into a covenant to seek the LORD God of their fathers with all their heart and with all their soul;", insight:"The covenant was wholehearted seeking. God asks for all the heart, not a portion."},
{q:"What was decreed for whoever would not seek the LORD?", options:["They should be put to death, whether small or great, man or woman","They should be exiled to Egypt","They should pay double tribute","They should be barred from the temple"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:13",verse:"That whosoever would not seek the LORD God of Israel should be put to death, whether small or great, whether man or woman.", insight:"The decree sounds severe because covenant was serious. Halfhearted faith was treated as treason against the King of kings."},
{q:"How did the people swear unto the LORD?", options:["With a loud voice, and with shouting, and with trumpets, and with cornets","In silence, with bowed heads","With fasting for seven days","Through the priests alone"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:14",verse:"And they sware unto the LORD with a loud voice, and with shouting, and with trumpets, and with cornets.", insight:"Their oath was loud and joyful, not grim. Covenant with God was a celebration, not a chore."},
{q:"Why did all Judah rejoice at the oath?", options:["They had sworn with all their heart, and sought him with their whole desire, and he was found of them","Shishak had been defeated","The temple was finished","Jeroboam had died"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:15",verse:"And all Judah rejoiced at the oath: for they had sworn with all their heart, and sought him with their whole desire; and he was found of them: and the LORD gave them rest round about.", insight:"Joy followed wholeheartedness. God gave them rest round about because they had given Him their whole desire."},
{q:"Why did Asa remove Maachah from being queen mother?", options:["She had made an idol in a grove","She conspired with Jeroboam","She refused the covenant","She stole temple gold"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:16",verse:"And also concerning Maachah the mother of Asa the king, he removed her from being queen, because she had made an idol in a grove: and Asa cut down her idol, and stamped it, and burnt it at the brook Kidron.", insight:"Asa put God above family ties. Obedience that stops at the palace door is not obedience."},
{q:"What did Asa do with Maachah's idol?", options:["He cut it down, stamped it, and burnt it at the brook Kidron","He sold it to the merchants","He buried it under the altar","He sent it back to her family"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:16",verse:"And also concerning Maachah the mother of Asa the king, he removed her from being queen, because she had made an idol in a grove: and Asa cut down her idol, and stamped it, and burnt it at the brook Kidron.", insight:"Asa destroyed the idol completely. Half measures with idols leave room for them to return."},
{q:"How long was there no more war in Asa's reign?", options:["Unto the five and thirtieth year","Unto the twentieth year","Unto the fortieth year","For ten years only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 15:19",verse:"And there was no more war unto the five and thirtieth year of the reign of Asa.", insight:"Covenant faithfulness bought a generation of peace. Seeking God with the whole heart is the surest national defense."}
]}
]);
